## Dive into the Dangers: 103 Class I Recalls This Year and Counting

### Recall Breakdown

This week, public safety agencies announced several recalls that left food enthusiasts like us raising an eyebrow. Among the notable concerns, Silver Moon LP dba Loard's Ice Cream contributed to four recalls for undeclared allergens, including soy, milk, and Red #40 coloring. The products included Banana Ice Cream, Chocolate Showers Ice Cream, and more. Mislabeling allergens is not just a blooper; it's a serious health risk for consumers with sensitivities.

California Dairies Inc. issued Class I recalls on their milk powder products due to potential Salmonella contamination. These were distributed under five labels and packaged in bulk quantities--yet another example of potential cross-contamination at a large scale.

Other recalls of note include RAW FARM's cheddar cheese linked to E. coli outbreaks, and creamed honey from Walker Honey Co, which may contain harmful foreign materials like stainless steel dust and plastic shreds. While Lundberg Family Farms had issues with their Jasmine rice being contaminated with foreign materials, and SunRidge Farms faced pesticide residues in their chili bean blend.

### Classifying the Chaos

Recalls are classified by their risk to consumer health. This week's recalls include:

- Class I: 3 cases (California Dairies Inc., RAW FARM LLC, and Griffith Foods Inc. for Salmonella and E. coli)
- Class II: 8 cases (Silver Moon LP, Horizon Organic Dairy, and others for allergens and foreign objects)
- Class III: 1 case (Falcon Trading Co. for pesticide residues)

### Serial Offenders

One name that keeps cropping up is Silver Moon LP, which has been involved in multiple recalls over the past year, primarily due to allergen mislabeling.

But the data doesn't stop there. Year-to-date, 419 recalls have been reported across all classes. Of these, 25% are classified as Class I, with Salmonella and allergen contamination leading the way.

### Monthly Trends

The recalls keep coming. The current month alone registered an additional 92 recalls, showing an upward trend compared to the month before, which saw 84. At this rate, we might break new records in food safety incidents for the year.

### What's Next?

With repeat offenders piling up on the list, it's clear that some manufacturers need a long, hard look at their quality assurance processes. Consumers, stay vigilant and always check for recalls of products you use.

### Key Takeaways

- Stay informed: Regularly check for updates on food recalls.
- Know your brands: Questionable repeat offenders like Silver Moon LP warrant extra caution.
- Advocate for change: Consumers have the power to demand higher standards.

This week was a bitter reminder that food safety can't be taken for granted. Keep your eyes peeled, and your pantries reassured.
